var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"man/symmetries/#Symmetries","page":"Symmetries","text":"The package includes in-place symmetry operations for Fourier fields.","title":"Symmetries"},{"category":"section","location":"man/symmetries/#Shifts-And-Rotations","page":"Symmetries","text":"xshift! applies a continuous periodic shift in the x direction. yshift! applies the discrete y-shift symmetry associated with the default Kolmogorov forcing wavenumber. rotate! applies the half-turn/conjugation symmetry.\n\nThe composed helpers shift! and shiftrotate! are useful when comparing states modulo continuous and discrete symmetries.","title":"Shifts And Rotations"},{"category":"section","location":"man/symmetries/#Distances-And-Norms","page":"Symmetries","text":"normdiff and minnormdiff compare Fourier states. The symmetry-aware minimum distance searches over streamwise shifts and the package's discrete shift/rotation operations.","title":"Distances And Norms"},{"category":"section","location":"man/symmetries/#Diagnostics","page":"Symmetries","text":"laminarflow constructs the analytic laminar vorticity field for a chosen Reynolds number. dissrate and powinput evaluate standard flow diagnostics. radial_mean groups Fourier coefficients by radial wavenumber shells.","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"man/fields/#Fields","page":"Fields","text":"The package has two primary field containers:\n\nField stores a real physical-space scalar field.\nFTField stores a complex Fourier-space field in packed rfft layout.\n\nBoth behave like Julia arrays. Use parent(field) when direct access to the underlying storage is needed.","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"man/fields/#Physical-Fields","page":"Fields","text":"Field(m) allocates a zero field on a uniform grid with 2m+2 points in each direction. make_grid returns broadcastable coordinate arrays for evaluating analytic functions on this grid.\n\nu = Field(8) do x, y\n    sin(x) * cos(y)\nend","title":"Physical Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"man/fields/#Fourier-Fields","page":"Fields","text":"FTField(n, m) allocates Fourier storage with active cutoff n and storage cutoff m. Individual modes are indexed through WaveNumber:\n\nΩ = FTField(8, up_dealias_size(8))\nΩ[WaveNumber(2, 1)] = 1im\n\nThe helper growto! copies active coefficients into larger Fourier storage and clears inactive modes. This is useful when moving between active and dealiased representations.","title":"Fourier Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"man/fields/#Transforms","page":"Fields","text":"ForwardFFT! and InverseFFT! build reusable FFTW plans. The allocating helpers FFT and IFFT are convenient for one-off conversions or tests.\n\nForward transforms normalise by the number of physical grid points and apply the active mask. Inverse transforms mask the input before calling FFTW, which matches the solver convention that inactive storage modes are scratch space.","title":"Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"man/fields/#Spectral-Operators","page":"Fields","text":"The basic spectral operators are:\n\nddx! and ddy! for first derivatives.\nlaplacian! for Delta.\ninvlaplacian! for the zero-mean inverse elliptic operators.\n\nAll of these are diagonal in Fourier space and require matching input/output resolutions.","title":"Spectral Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"man/conventions/#Assumptions","page":"Assumptions","text":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.jl works with a two-dimensional vorticity formulation on the periodic square 02pi)^2. Physical fields are sampled on a uniform (2m+2) times (2m+2) grid, and Fourier fields use the packed layout produced by real-to-complex FFTs.","title":"Assumptions"},{"category":"section","location":"man/conventions/#Resolution-Parameters","page":"Assumptions","text":"Two integer cutoffs appear throughout the API:\n\nn is the active spectral cutoff. Solver loops evolve modes with j = 0ldotsn and k = -nldotsn.\nm is the storage cutoff. The physical grid has 2m+2 points per direction, and the stored Fourier array has size (2m+2, m+2).\n\nThe storage cutoff may be larger than the active cutoff. This is how the package represents dealiased nonlinear products: evolve the active modes, use a larger physical grid for products, and mask inactive modes after transforming back to Fourier space.","title":"Resolution Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"man/conventions/#Coordinates-And-Wavenumbers","page":"Assumptions","text":"Fourier modes are addressed with WaveNumber. The package convention is:\n\nWaveNumber(k, j) stores the signed full-FFT direction in k.\nj is the non-negative real-to-complex direction.\nNegative j modes are represented implicitly by Hermitian symmetry.\n\nThe differential operators follow this convention. ddx! multiplies by i j and ddy! multiplies by i k.","title":"Coordinates And Wavenumbers"},{"category":"section","location":"man/conventions/#Mutating-Operators","page":"Assumptions","text":"Most expensive operations are written as f!(out, in) and return out. This style is used for transforms, derivatives, elliptic solves, shifts, and time-stepping terms. Solver objects such as ForwardEquation and LinearisedEquation own their work arrays and FFT plans so repeated calls can be allocation-conscious.","title":"Mutating Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"man/conventions/#Mean-And-Symmetry","page":"Assumptions","text":"The zero mode is treated specially in several operators. Inverse Laplacians set the mean to zero, and FFT plans apply masking and Hermitian cleanup so inactive or redundant modes do not leak into the active dynamics.","title":"Mean And Symmetry"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<p align=\"center\">\n  <img src=\"assets/logo.png\" alt=\"OpenKolmogorovFlow.jl logo\" width=\"360\">\n</p>","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#OpenKolmogorovFlow.jl","page":"Home","text":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.jl contains spectral solver utilities for the two-dimensional open Kolmogorov-flow vorticity equation on a periodic square. It provides field containers, Fourier transforms, differential operators, forward/tangent/adjoint equations, forcing objects, diagnostics, and symmetry maps.\n\nThe package is deliberately small and operator-oriented. Most numerical operations are mutating, preallocated functions with names ending in !, which lets time-stepping code reuse storage instead of allocating new arrays at every stage.","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Assumptions: domain, Fourier layout, truncation, and mutating conventions.\nFields: physical fields, Fourier fields, grids, transforms, and dealiasing.\nEquations: forward, tangent, adjoint, implicit, and forcing objects.\nSymmetries: shifts, rotations, diagnostics, and spectra.\nReference: the documented public API.","title":"Contents"},{"category":"module","location":"#OpenKolmogorovFlow.OpenKolmogorovFlow","page":"Home","text":"OpenKolmogorovFlow\n\nSpectral solver utilities for the two-dimensional open Kolmogorov-flow vorticity equation on a periodic square domain.\n\nThe package stores real-space fields on a uniform (2m+2)  (2m+2) grid and stores vorticity in real-to-complex Fourier layout through FTField. The logical active cutoff n controls which Fourier modes are evolved, while the storage cutoff m may be larger to provide dealiasing. Operators, nonlinear terms, tangent equations, adjoint equations, forcings, symmetries, and CFL-based timestep hooks are all built around this pair of resolutions.\n\nMost mutating functions follow Julia's f!(out, in) convention and return the modified output. Many solver objects carry preallocated work arrays so repeated time stepping can avoid transient allocations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.OpenKolmogorovFlow"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Indexing","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Indexing"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Fields","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Fields"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#FFTs","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"FFTs"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Operators","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Operators"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Forward-Equation","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Forward Equation"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Linearised-Equations","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Linearised Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Forcings","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Forcings"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Hooks","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Hooks"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Diagnostics","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Norms","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Norms"},{"category":"section","location":"man/api/#Symmetries","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Symmetries"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.WaveNumber","page":"Reference","text":"WaveNumber(k, j)\n\nFourier-mode identifier used for indexing FTField.\n\nk is the signed wavenumber stored along the full complex FFT direction and j is the non-negative wavenumber stored along the real-to-complex FFT direction. Because the package stores an rfft half-plane, negative j modes are represented implicitly by Hermitian symmetry and are not valid indices.\n\nExample\n\nΩ = FTField(4, 6)\nΩ[WaveNumber(-2, 1)] = 1 + 2im\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.WaveNumber"},{"category":"macro","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.@loop_k","page":"Reference","text":"@loop_k n m expr\n\nLoop over the signed active k wavenumbers -n:n while exposing both k and the storage index _k inside expr.\n\nThis macro is an internal convenience for allocation-free spectral loops. It keeps the signed wavenumber (k) available for formulas and the corresponding stored row (_k) available for direct FTField indexing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.@loop_k"},{"category":"macro","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.@loop_jk","page":"Reference","text":"@loop_jk n m expr\n\nLoop over all explicitly stored active Fourier modes.\n\nInside expr, j and k are physical wavenumbers and _j, _k are their storage indices. The loop covers j = 0:n and k = -n:n, matching the package's active Fourier set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.@loop_jk"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractField","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractField{m,T} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nAbstract matrix-like representation of a real physical-space field.\n\nThe type parameter m determines the uniform grid size 2m+2 in both periodic directions. Concrete fields are stored as ordinary matrices in the same orientation used by FFTW plans in this package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractField"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.Field","page":"Reference","text":"Field(data::AbstractMatrix{<:Real})\nField(m::Int, [T=Float64])\nField(m::Int, fun)\n\nPhysical-space scalar field on the periodic square.\n\nField(data) wraps a square, even-sized real matrix. The grid parameter is inferred from size(data,1) == 2m+2. Field(m, T) allocates a zero field, and Field(m, fun) evaluates fun.(x, y) on make_grid.\n\nThe object behaves as an AbstractMatrix; use parent(u) to access the underlying storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.Field"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.make_grid","page":"Reference","text":"make_grid(m::Int) -> (x, y)\nmake_grid(u::Field) -> (x, y)\n\nReturn broadcastable coordinate arrays for the periodic square [0, 2π)^2.\n\nBoth directions have 2m+2 points. The endpoint 2π is omitted so periodic functions are sampled without duplicating the first point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.make_grid"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractFTField","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractFTField{n,m,T} <: AbstractMatrix{Complex{T}}\n\nAbstract Fourier-space field with active cutoff n and storage cutoff m.\n\nThe field stores a real-to-complex Fourier half-plane of size (2m+2, m+2). Only modes with j = 0:n and k = -n:n are considered active by the solver. The remaining stored modes are available for dealiasing and are normally kept zero by FFT masking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractFTField"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.FTField","page":"Reference","text":"FTField(n::Int, m::Int, [T=Float64])\nFTField(data::AbstractMatrix{Complex{T}}, n::Int)\n\nFourier-space vorticity field in packed rfft layout.\n\nn is the largest active wavenumber used by spectral loops. m is the storage resolution and must satisfy m >= n. A larger m is used for dealiasing the nonlinear term while preserving an active mode set of size n.\n\nModes are usually accessed with WaveNumber:\n\nΩ = FTField(8, up_dealias_size(8))\nΩ[WaveNumber(2, 1)] = 0.5im\n\nThe zero and Nyquist-related modes are handled by the FFT masking/symmetry helpers; direct low-level edits should preserve the package's Hermitian symmetry assumptions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.FTField"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.growto!","page":"Reference","text":"growto!(OUT, U) -> OUT\n\nCopy the active Fourier coefficients of U into OUT.\n\nOUT must have storage resolution at least as large as U and an active cutoff at least as large as U's active cutoff. All coefficients of OUT are cleared before active modes are copied, so inactive/dealiased modes are zero in the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.growto!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.up_dealias_size","page":"Reference","text":"up_dealias_size(n::Int) -> Int\n\nReturn the storage cutoff m used for the 3/2-rule dealiased grid associated with active cutoff n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.up_dealias_size"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.down_dealias_size","page":"Reference","text":"down_dealias_size(m::Int) -> Int\n\nReturn the largest active cutoff n whose 3/2-rule storage cutoff fits inside an existing physical-space storage cutoff m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.down_dealias_size"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ForwardFFT!","page":"Reference","text":"ForwardFFT!(u::AbstractField; flags=FFTW.EXHAUSTIVE)\n\nReusable in-place forward FFT plan from Field to FTField.\n\nCalling the plan writes Fourier coefficients into the supplied FTField, normalises by the number of physical grid points, applies the active-mode mask, and restores the stored Hermitian symmetry on the zero j column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ForwardFFT!"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.InverseFFT!","page":"Reference","text":"InverseFFT!(U::AbstractFTField; flags=FFTW.EXHAUSTIVE)\n\nReusable in-place inverse FFT plan from FTField to Field.\n\nThe input Fourier field is masked before FFTW executes. This is appropriate for solver caches where inactive modes are scratch storage; callers that need to preserve every stored coefficient should pass a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.InverseFFT!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.FFT","page":"Reference","text":"FFT(u::AbstractField, n::Int) -> FTField\n\nAllocate a Fourier field with active cutoff n and transform the physical field u into it.\n\nThe storage cutoff is inherited from u, so u::Field{m} produces FTField{n,m}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.FFT"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.IFFT","page":"Reference","text":"IFFT(U::AbstractFTField) -> Field\n\nAllocate a physical-space field and inverse transform U into it.\n\nThe returned field has the storage cutoff m of U.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.IFFT"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ddx!","page":"Reference","text":"ddx!(OUT, U) -> OUT\n\nDifferentiate the Fourier-space field U along the x direction.\n\nThe derivative is applied spectrally by multiplying each active mode by im*j, following the package convention that j is the non-negative real-to-complex direction. OUT and U must have matching active and storage resolutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ddx!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ddy!","page":"Reference","text":"ddy!(OUT, U) -> OUT\n\nDifferentiate the Fourier-space field U along the y direction.\n\nThe derivative is applied spectrally by multiplying each active mode by im*k, where k is the signed full-FFT wavenumber.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ddy!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.laplacian!","page":"Reference","text":"laplacian!(OUT, U) -> OUT\n\nApply the spectral Laplacian to U.\n\nEach active Fourier coefficient is multiplied by -(j^2 + k^2). The zero mode is therefore mapped to zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.laplacian!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.invlaplacian!","page":"Reference","text":"invlaplacian!(OUT, U) -> OUT\ninvlaplacian!(OUT, U, c) -> OUT\n\nApply a diagonal inverse elliptic operator in Fourier space.\n\nWithout c, this computes -Δ^-1 U mode-by-mode and sets the mean mode to zero. With c, this computes (I - cΔ)^-1 U and also zeros the mean mode, matching the implicit time-stepping operator used by Flows.ImcA!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.invlaplacian!"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ForwardExplicitTerm","page":"Reference","text":"ForwardExplicitTerm(n, m, kforcing, flags, T)\n\nPreallocated explicit nonlinear term for the forward vorticity equation.\n\nThis object owns Fourier and physical work arrays, plus reusable FFT plans. It computes advection, updates the CFL estimate β, and adds the built-in Kolmogorov forcing modes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ForwardExplicitTerm"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ForwardEquation","page":"Reference","text":"ForwardEquation(n, m, Re[, kforcing=4, flags=FFTW.EXHAUSTIVE,\n                forcing=DummyForcing(n), T=Float64])\n\nForward vorticity equation for open Kolmogorov flow.\n\nThe equation combines the implicit viscous term, the explicit nonlinear advection and built-in Kolmogorov forcing, and an optional user-supplied forcing object. It follows the callable interface expected by Flows.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ForwardEquation"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.splitexim","page":"Reference","text":"splitexim(eq::ForwardEquation) -> (explicit, implicit)\n\nReturn the explicit and implicit pieces of a ForwardEquation.\n\nThe returned explicit closure has the signature expected by IMEX integrators in Flows.jl; the returned implicit term is the equation's ImplicitTerm.\n\n\n\n\n\nsplitexim(eq::LinearisedEquation) -> (explicit, implicit)\n\nReturn explicit and implicit pieces for Flows.jl split integration.\n\nThe returned explicit closure supports both the coupled tangent signature (t, Ω, dΩdt, Λ, dΛdt, add) and the adjoint-only signature (t, Ω, Λ, dΛdt, add).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.splitexim"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ImplicitTerm","page":"Reference","text":"ImplicitTerm(Re)\n\nViscous linear term for the vorticity equation.\n\nmul!(OUT, ImplicitTerm(Re), U) computes ΔU / Re in Fourier space. The type is mutable so continuation or parameter studies can adjust Re without rebuilding the whole equation object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ImplicitTerm"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractLinearMode","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractLinearMode\n\nMarker supertype for choosing tangent or adjoint linearised dynamics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractLinearMode"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.TangentMode","page":"Reference","text":"TangentMode()\n\nSelect the tangent linear equation about a supplied forward trajectory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.TangentMode"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.AdjointMode","page":"Reference","text":"AdjointMode()\n\nSelect the adjoint linear equation associated with the package inner product.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.AdjointMode"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.LinearisedExTerm","page":"Reference","text":"LinearisedExTerm(n, m, mode, T, flags)\n\nPreallocated explicit term for tangent or adjoint vorticity dynamics.\n\nThe mode argument is either TangentMode or AdjointMode. The object owns all Fourier/physical work arrays required to evaluate the linearised nonlinear term without allocating in the main time-stepping loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.LinearisedExTerm"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.LinearisedEquation","page":"Reference","text":"LinearisedEquation(n, m, Re, mode[, flags=FFTW.EXHAUSTIVE,\n                  forcing=DummyForcing(n), T=Float64])\n\nTangent or adjoint linearised equation about a forward vorticity trajectory.\n\nmode selects whether the equation evolves perturbations forward in tangent form or adjoint variables backward/with adjoint integrators. The object follows the Flows.jl callable interface used by coupled and split IMEX integration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.LinearisedEquation"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractForcing","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractForcing{n}\n\nSupertype for additive forcing objects acting on fields with active cutoff n.\n\nForcing objects are callable and mutate the right-hand-side field passed as the last argument. Implementations may support the forward, tangent, or adjoint equation signatures depending on where the forcing is meaningful.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.AbstractForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.DummyForcing","page":"Reference","text":"DummyForcing(n)\n\nNo-op forcing with active cutoff n.\n\nUse this as the default forcing object when the equation should only include its built-in terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.DummyForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.SteadyForcing","page":"Reference","text":"SteadyForcing(f)\n\nTime-independent additive forcing stored as an FTField.\n\nCalling the forcing adds f to the destination right-hand side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.SteadyForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.WaveNumberForcing","page":"Reference","text":"WaveNumberForcing(n, k, j, value)\n\nAdd a forcing at one Fourier mode.\n\nFor j == 0, the conjugate -k mode is also updated so the physical-space forcing remains real. The forcing object can be used with direct, tangent, and adjoint equation call signatures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.WaveNumberForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.DissRateGradientForcing","page":"Reference","text":"DissRateGradientForcing(n, Re)\n\nAdjoint forcing corresponding to the gradient of dissipation rate.\n\nWhen called on an adjoint right-hand side, this subtracts 2Ω/Re.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.DissRateGradientForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.ReForcing","page":"Reference","text":"ReForcing(n, m, Re)\nReForcing(Re, TMP)\n\nTangent-equation forcing for a perturbation with respect to Reynolds number.\n\nThe object owns a temporary Fourier field TMP used to apply the Laplacian of the base vorticity before adding the parameter derivative contribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.ReForcing"},{"category":"type","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.CFLHook","page":"Reference","text":"CFLHook([CFL_max=1, Δt_min=0, Δt_max=1])\n\nTime-step selection hook based on the CFL estimate stored by the explicit term.\n\nThe hook returns a clamped time step in the interval (Δt_min, Δt_max). For a single ForwardExplicitTerm, it reads the most recent advective time scale from β[1]; for a Flows.Coupled object, it uses the first equation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.CFLHook"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.laminarflow","page":"Reference","text":"laminarflow(n, m, Re, [kforcing=4]) -> FTField\n\nReturn the steady laminar vorticity field for the sinusoidal Kolmogorov forcing.\n\nThe returned field has active cutoff n, storage cutoff m, and nonzero coefficients only at WaveNumber(±kforcing, 0). The forcing wavenumber must be representable in the active mode set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.laminarflow"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.dissrate","page":"Reference","text":"dissrate(Ω, Re) -> Real\n\nEnergy-dissipation-rate density associated with vorticity field Ω.\n\nThe value is computed as norm(Ω)^2 / Re using the package's Fourier-space inner product normalization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.dissrate"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.powinput","page":"Reference","text":"powinput(Ω, [kf=4]) -> Real\n\nReturn the power input associated with the active forcing mode kf.\n\nThis diagnostic reads the vorticity coefficient at WaveNumber(kf, 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.powinput"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.radial_mean!","page":"Reference","text":"radial_mean!(f, Ω, out, counts) -> out\n\nCompute a radial average of f applied to Fourier coefficients.\n\nout[r] accumulates the average over modes whose radius sqrt(j^2 + k^2) rounds to integer shell r. counts is a work vector used to store the number of modes in each shell. Both vectors are overwritten.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.radial_mean!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.radial_mean","page":"Reference","text":"radial_mean(f, Ω) -> Vector\n\nAllocating wrapper around radial_mean!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.radial_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.normdiff","page":"Reference","text":"normdiff(Ω₁, Ω₂) -> Real\n\nReturn the squared norm of Ω₁ - Ω₂ using the same Fourier weights as the Fourier-space dot method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.normdiff"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.minnormdiff","page":"Reference","text":"minnormdiff(Ω₁, Ω₂[, TMP, N]) -> (distance, (xshift, yshift_index))\n\nScan over package symmetries and return the smallest squared distance between shifted copies of Ω₁ and Ω₂.\n\nN controls the number of trial shifts in the continuous x direction. The integer yshift_index is passed to yshift!, corresponding to quarter period shifts of the default kforcing = 4 symmetry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.minnormdiff"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.xshift!","page":"Reference","text":"xshift!(U, s) -> U\n\nApply a continuous periodic shift by distance s in the x direction.\n\nThe operation is diagonal in Fourier space and mutates U in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.xshift!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.yshift!","page":"Reference","text":"yshift!(U, m) -> U\n\nApply the discrete y-shift symmetry used by the default Kolmogorov forcing.\n\nm is an integer quarter-period index; the implementation assumes the forcing wavenumber kf = 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.yshift!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.yshiftreflect!","page":"Reference","text":"yshiftreflect!(U) -> U\n\nApply the package's combined vertical shift/reflection symmetry in place.\n\nThis helper assumes the default kf = 4 symmetry class.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.yshiftreflect!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.rotate!","page":"Reference","text":"rotate!(U) -> U\n\nApply the half-turn/conjugation symmetry in Fourier space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.rotate!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.shift!","page":"Reference","text":"shift!(U, s, m) -> U\n\nApply xshift!(U, s) followed by yshift!(U, m).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.shift!"},{"category":"function","location":"man/api/#OpenKolmogorovFlow.shiftrotate!","page":"Reference","text":"shiftrotate!(U, s, m) -> U\n\nApply shift! followed by rotate!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.shiftrotate!"},{"category":"section","location":"man/equations/#Equations","page":"Equations","text":"OpenKolmogorovFlow.jl represents equations as callable objects compatible with the Flows.jl time-stepping interface.","title":"Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"man/equations/#Forward-Equation","page":"Equations","text":"ForwardEquation combines three pieces:\n\nan ImplicitTerm for viscous diffusion;\na ForwardExplicitTerm for nonlinear advection and built-in Kolmogorov forcing;\nan optional AbstractForcing object for extra forcing.\n\nThe nonlinear term computes derivatives and velocities spectrally, transforms to physical space for products, then transforms the result back to Fourier space. The explicit term also updates the CFL estimate used by CFLHook.","title":"Forward Equation"},{"category":"section","location":"man/equations/#Split-Integration","page":"Equations","text":"splitexim returns explicit and implicit pieces for IMEX methods. The explicit closure mutates the supplied right-hand side, while the implicit term implements multiplication and shifted inversion for the linear viscous part.","title":"Split Integration"},{"category":"section","location":"man/equations/#Tangent-And-Adjoint-Equations","page":"Equations","text":"LinearisedEquation uses TangentMode or AdjointMode to choose the linearised operator. The tangent equation evolves perturbations about a forward state. The adjoint equation uses the package inner product and supports adjoint forcing objects such as DissRateGradientForcing.","title":"Tangent And Adjoint Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"man/equations/#Forcing-Objects","page":"Equations","text":"Forcings are small callable objects that mutate the destination right-hand side:\n\nDummyForcing leaves the right-hand side unchanged.\nSteadyForcing adds a stored Fourier field.\nWaveNumberForcing adds a mode-local forcing.\nReForcing provides the tangent forcing associated with a Reynolds number perturbation.\nDissRateGradientForcing provides the adjoint forcing for dissipation-rate gradients.","title":"Forcing Objects"}]
}
